1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,867
This is the Assignment 2 demo: HTTP APIs, gRPC and metrics, built on the Gym Tracker

2
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:08,264
from Assignment 1.

3
00:00:08,544 --> 00:00:11,744
The user task is logging one set during a live workout.

4
00:00:12,024 --> 00:00:18,360
Over HTTP it is a POST to a workout's sets; over gRPC it is WorkoutService.LogSet,

5
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:20,600
served by a separate process.

6
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:25,856
We go through Part A, the HTTP API; Part B, the gRPC service;

7
00:00:25,856 --> 00:00:27,814
and Part C, the metrics.

8
00:00:28,994 --> 00:00:31,447
Everything runs locally with Docker Compose.

9
00:00:31,727 --> 00:00:37,459
Beside the app container, gym-tracker, is the new gym-grpc container, both on the same

10
00:00:37,459 --> 00:00:42,457
Postgres, with Prometheus, Grafana and the Elastic stack from Assignment 1.

11
00:00:43,637 --> 00:00:45,835
Part A starts with the six REST constraints.

12
00:00:46,115 --> 00:00:51,032
Client-server: the browser app and the Python caller share only the JSON contract with

13
00:00:51,032 --> 00:00:51,661
the server.

14
00:00:51,941 --> 00:00:56,571
Stateless: every request carries its credentials and names its resource in the path.

15
00:00:56,851 --> 00:01:00,083
Cacheable, partly: static files carry Cache-Control;

16
00:01:00,083 --> 00:01:02,632
per-user JSON is deliberately not cached.

17
00:01:02,912 --> 00:01:08,267
Uniform interface: plural nouns, standard methods, real status codes, one error body.

18
00:01:08,547 --> 00:01:13,837
Layered system: clients only know a base URL, so a proxy in between changes nothing.

19
00:01:14,117 --> 00:01:19,702
And code on demand, the optional one, is shown: the browser runs the app.js and service

20
00:01:19,702 --> 00:01:21,243
worker the server sends.

21
00:01:22,423 --> 00:01:26,583
Every endpoint under /api was reviewed, leaving out health and metrics.

22
00:01:26,863 --> 00:01:30,703
For logging a set, this table compares the old endpoint with the new one.

23
00:01:30,983 --> 00:01:36,190
The singular path became the plural /api/sessions, with the old path kept as a

24
00:01:36,190 --> 00:01:38,727
deprecated alias for installed phones.

25
00:01:39,007 --> 00:01:44,641
Untyped dictionaries became typed models, so bad input is a 400 before any database

26
00:01:44,641 --> 00:01:44,980
work.

27
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:51,276
Errors now use 400, 401, 404, 409, 429 or 500,

28
00:01:51,556 --> 00:01:55,204
always in one body with a code, a message and the request id.

29
00:01:55,484 --> 00:01:58,236
And an Idempotency-Key header makes retries safe.

30
00:01:58,516 --> 00:02:03,846
Elsewhere, /session became /sessions, PUT became PATCH, the rebuild endpoint gained a

31
00:02:03,846 --> 00:02:08,799
rate limit, and the rest were left alone. The browser app was updated to match.

32
00:02:09,979 --> 00:02:16,196
The OpenAPI Specification is a standard, machine-readable description of an HTTP API:

33
00:02:16,196 --> 00:02:21,243
its paths, methods, parameters, bodies, responses and authentication.

34
00:02:21,523 --> 00:02:26,876
Tools read it to render documentation, generate clients, and test requests, so it is the

35
00:02:26,876 --> 00:02:29,309
contract between an API and its callers.

36
00:02:29,589 --> 00:02:34,365
Here it is generated from the code, and a test fails if the committed file and the code

37
00:02:34,365 --> 00:02:34,859
disagree.

38
00:02:35,875 --> 00:02:40,397
Swagger UI renders the spec, with every route, its inputs and its responses.

39
00:02:40,677 --> 00:02:44,261
We authorize with the demo account and try the log-a-set endpoint.

40
00:02:49,859 --> 00:02:53,482
The request has an Idempotency-Key header and a body:

41
00:02:53,482 --> 00:02:55,875
the exercise, 80 kilograms, 8 reps.

42
00:02:58,747 --> 00:03:02,779
The answer is 201 Created, with the new set and its log id.

43
00:03:03,059 --> 00:03:05,278
The same request again, with the same key,

44
00:03:07,845 --> 00:03:12,143
returns 200, with idempotent-replayed: true and the same log id.

45
00:03:12,143 --> 00:03:13,755
Nothing new was written.

46
00:03:14,035 --> 00:03:15,379
With reps set to zero,

47
00:03:22,385 --> 00:03:27,803
the answer is 400, in the shared error format: a code, a message and the request id.

48
00:03:28,983 --> 00:03:34,199
Logging a set is what gets retried: on gym wifi, a response can be lost after the set

49
00:03:34,199 --> 00:03:34,935
was written.

50
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:39,034
So the client sends one key per set, unchanged on every retry.

51
00:03:39,314 --> 00:03:43,983
The server stores it under a unique index per account, looks it up first, and returns

52
00:03:43,983 --> 00:03:46,290
the stored set instead of writing another.

53
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:50,026
Two copies racing past the lookup are settled by that index.

54
00:03:50,306 --> 00:03:55,479
Other calls are safe to repeat by design: starting a workout returns the live one, and

55
00:03:55,479 --> 00:03:58,306
rebuilding records twice gives the same result.

56
00:03:59,486 --> 00:04:04,073
Rate limiting protects POST /api/records/rebuild, the most expensive call.

57
00:04:04,353 --> 00:04:07,723
The limit is three calls per sixty seconds, in a sliding window,

58
00:04:08,003 --> 00:04:11,587
shared by everything one account does; other users are unaffected.

59
00:04:11,867 --> 00:04:15,985
Every attempt counts, and Retry-After says when a call will be accepted again.

60
00:04:24,155 --> 00:04:29,403
Five calls: three are accepted, and the fourth and fifth get 429 Too Many Requests.

61
00:04:29,683 --> 00:04:33,779
The full response has Retry-After: 60 and the standard error body.

62
00:04:34,803 --> 00:04:38,280
After waiting those 60 seconds, the call is accepted again.

63
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:43,620
The tests cover a valid request, invalid input and a repeated request.

64
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:49,766
201 for a valid set, 400 in the shared shape for bad input, and one row for a repeat,

65
00:04:49,766 --> 00:04:53,010
even with two copies racing on a real Postgres.

66
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:55,444
All 56 Assignment 2 tests pass,

67
00:04:57,495 --> 00:05:00,759
and the committed OpenAPI document matches the code.

68
00:05:01,939 --> 00:05:06,939
The Python caller plays another service, say a watch app's sync job, logging several

69
00:05:06,939 --> 00:05:07,891
sets in one run.

70
00:05:08,171 --> 00:05:10,411
Each attempt times out after 2 seconds.

71
00:05:10,691 --> 00:05:15,315
A failure is retried at most twice, with backoff from half a second, doubling, plus

72
00:05:15,315 --> 00:05:15,705
jitter.

73
00:05:15,985 --> 00:05:21,115
Only timeouts, connection errors and 5xx are retried, never a 4xx, and the

74
00:05:21,115 --> 00:05:24,305
Idempotency-Key makes retrying this POST safe.

75
00:05:24,585 --> 00:05:29,662
The circuit breaker opens after three consecutive failures and allows one trial call

76
00:05:29,662 --> 00:05:30,750
after ten seconds.

77
00:05:31,930 --> 00:05:36,811
The demo restarts the app so that every fourth request is held for three seconds, longer

78
00:05:36,811 --> 00:05:37,754
than the timeout.

79
00:05:38,034 --> 00:05:39,229
The first sets succeed.

80
00:05:40,576 --> 00:05:43,242
Set four times out, and succeeds on the retry.

81
00:05:43,522 --> 00:05:48,353
Then the app container is stopped. Set six is refused three times, and the breaker

82
00:05:48,353 --> 00:05:48,706
opens.

83
00:05:48,986 --> 00:05:52,016
Now calls are blocked at once, without touching the network.

84
00:05:54,283 --> 00:05:58,528
The first trial fails, since the app is still down, so the breaker opens again.

85
00:06:00,752 --> 00:06:04,464
With the container back, the next trial succeeds and the breaker closes.

86
00:06:04,744 --> 00:06:08,456
Ten sets written, nineteen blocked, one failed, none written twice.

87
00:06:09,636 --> 00:06:14,436
Part B serves one operation, logging a set, from a new gRPC service.

88
00:06:14,716 --> 00:06:22,248
It runs in its own container, gym-grpc, on port 50051, with metrics on 9102.

89
00:06:22,248 --> 00:06:25,617
The HTTP version stays in the app.

90
00:06:25,897 --> 00:06:32,054
Both handlers call the same function, sessions.log_set, on the same database, so the

91
00:06:32,054 --> 00:06:37,844
business rules are one piece of code. The gRPC server never calls the HTTP API.

92
00:06:38,124 --> 00:06:41,622
It is a unary RPC: one set in, one result out.

93
00:06:42,802 --> 00:06:47,325
The contract is workout.proto: one service, one RPC, LogSet.

94
00:06:47,605 --> 00:06:53,239
Weight and reps are optional, so a missing value differs from zero, and the idempotency

95
00:06:53,239 --> 00:06:54,922
key follows the HTTP rule.

96
00:06:55,202 --> 00:06:58,829
The response has the set, any records it beat, and a replayed flag.

97
00:07:00,009 --> 00:07:03,102
The code is generated with grpc_tools.protoc,

98
00:07:03,382 --> 00:07:06,796
producing the messages, type hints and gRPC module,

99
00:07:07,076 --> 00:07:09,977
which holds the client stub and the servicer base class.

100
00:07:10,257 --> 00:07:13,414
Our handler subclasses the servicer and implements LogSet,

101
00:07:13,694 --> 00:07:17,129
and both handlers call the same sessions_module.log_set.

102
00:07:17,409 --> 00:07:20,118
Regenerating gives exactly the committed code.

103
00:07:21,298 --> 00:07:26,205
The gRPC client takes its input from flags and sets a two-second deadline.

104
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:34,756
A valid set returns OK, with the set, the records it beat, and our request id echoed

105
00:07:34,756 --> 00:07:35,072
back.

106
00:07:35,352 --> 00:07:40,088
Zero reps fails with INVALID_ARGUMENT and the same message as HTTP.

107
00:07:40,368 --> 00:07:43,419
And a one-millisecond deadline gives DEADLINE_EXCEEDED.

108
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:48,033
The traffic script sends the same four calls through both versions.

109
00:07:48,313 --> 00:07:55,524
HTTP: 201, then 200 replayed, 400 for zero reps, and 404 for a missing workout.

110
00:07:55,804 --> 00:08:00,412
gRPC: OK, OK replayed, INVALID_ARGUMENT and NOT_FOUND.

111
00:08:00,692 --> 00:08:06,452
Directly over HTTP, the failure is a 400 with our request id in the error body.

112
00:08:08,462 --> 00:08:12,985
Both map the same business exceptions, so an input fails the same way over each.

113
00:08:13,265 --> 00:08:17,742
HTTP tells created from replayed with 201 and 200;

114
00:08:17,742 --> 00:08:20,966
gRPC has one OK and a replayed flag.

115
00:08:21,246 --> 00:08:26,643
A bad field is 400 or INVALID_ARGUMENT: retrying the same input cannot help.

116
00:08:26,923 --> 00:08:32,939
A missing workout is 404 or NOT_FOUND; a finished one is 409 or FAILED_PRECONDITION.

117
00:08:33,219 --> 00:08:39,781
Bad credentials are 401 or UNAUTHENTICATED; a bug is 500 or INTERNAL, with details only

118
00:08:39,781 --> 00:08:40,686
in the logs.

119
00:08:40,966 --> 00:08:46,998
And a caller that stops waiting gets a client timeout over HTTP, but DEADLINE_EXCEEDED

120
00:08:46,998 --> 00:08:49,734
over gRPC, which also tells the server.

121
00:08:50,914 --> 00:08:52,301
Here are both call paths.

122
00:08:52,581 --> 00:08:58,838
HTTP: JSON to port 8000, the middleware that sets the request id and counts the request,

123
00:08:58,838 --> 00:09:01,754
then the handler that validates the body.

124
00:09:02,034 --> 00:09:08,860
gRPC: the client calls the generated stub, which sends protobuf over HTTP/2 to port

125
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:14,535
50051, where the generated servicer dispatches to our LogSet handler.

126
00:09:14,815 --> 00:09:18,403
Both end in sessions.log_set and the same Postgres table.

127
00:09:18,403 --> 00:09:19,914
Neither wraps the other.

128
00:09:20,999 --> 00:09:25,308
Logs and trace correlation still work across the gRPC boundary.

129
00:09:25,588 --> 00:09:31,647
The client sends x-request-id as metadata; the server logs under it and returns it, as

130
00:09:31,647 --> 00:09:34,676
the HTTP middleware does with X-Request-ID.

131
00:09:34,956 --> 00:09:37,431
So one Kibana search on our request ids

132
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:46,688
finds the gRPC lines from gym-tracker-grpc, set logged and rpc served, OK and

133
00:09:46,688 --> 00:09:51,408
INVALID_ARGUMENT, beside the HTTP failure from the app.

134
00:09:52,588 --> 00:09:55,788
Part C starts with an audit of the Assignment 1 metrics.

135
00:09:56,068 --> 00:10:02,830
The HTTP counter, duration histogram and in-flight gauge already cover the HTTP LogSet,

136
00:10:02,830 --> 00:10:05,007
429s included, so they stay.

137
00:10:05,287 --> 00:10:07,953
The gRPC service had no metrics at all.

138
00:10:08,233 --> 00:10:11,924
Live-workout sets were never counted, the gap Assignment 1 listed.

139
00:10:12,204 --> 00:10:14,231
And nothing showed idempotency working.

140
00:10:14,511 --> 00:10:17,391
Build info and the LLM gauge were left alone.

141
00:10:18,571 --> 00:10:19,915
Four metrics were added.

142
00:10:20,195 --> 00:10:25,891
A counter of gRPC calls by service, method and status code, for rate and errors.

143
00:10:26,171 --> 00:10:30,288
A duration histogram in seconds, for p50 and p95.

144
00:10:30,568 --> 00:10:32,040
A gauge of calls in flight.

145
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:36,459
And a counter of live sets by transport and outcome, written or replayed.

146
00:10:36,739 --> 00:10:40,877
All labels come from fixed lists; no user, request or trace ids.

147
00:10:42,057 --> 00:10:45,620
The gRPC service exposes them on its own endpoint,

148
00:10:45,900 --> 00:10:47,372
with only bounded labels,

149
00:10:49,262 --> 00:10:53,699
and buckets from five milliseconds to ten seconds, the same as HTTP.

150
00:10:54,879 --> 00:10:59,731
Duration is measured at the server for both transports, so network time is left out and

151
00:10:59,731 --> 00:11:01,237
they compare like for like.

152
00:11:01,517 --> 00:11:07,098
The buckets are dense from five to a hundred milliseconds, where a LogSet normally

153
00:11:07,098 --> 00:11:10,093
falls; p50 and p95 use a five-minute window.

154
00:11:10,373 --> 00:11:15,158
In-flight calls beat message size: a request is tens of bytes, a flat line, while

155
00:11:15,158 --> 00:11:18,821
concurrency is what runs out first, with eight worker threads.

156
00:11:19,101 --> 00:11:22,087
And the live-sets counter fills the Assignment 1 gap.

157
00:11:23,109 --> 00:11:28,400
Prometheus scrapes the gRPC service as its own job, and it is up beside the app.

158
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:34,120
The cardinality demo is down by design; it only runs for an Assignment 1 experiment.

159
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:38,453
Here is the gRPC p95 over a five-minute window.

160
00:11:42,524 --> 00:11:49,124
In Grafana, every gRPC panel sits beside its HTTP twin, filled by the repeatable traffic

161
00:11:49,124 --> 00:11:49,649
script.

162
00:11:49,929 --> 00:11:54,526
Calls per second and error ratios. About half the calls fail by design:

163
00:11:54,526 --> 00:11:57,310
invalid and not-found calls in every round.

164
00:11:57,590 --> 00:11:59,958
Calls by status code for each transport.

165
00:12:02,581 --> 00:12:08,366
Server-side p50 and p95 over five minutes; in this small virtual machine both sit at a

166
00:12:08,366 --> 00:12:10,048
few hundred milliseconds.

167
00:12:10,328 --> 00:12:13,229
In-flight calls, and live sets written versus replayed.

168
00:12:14,964 --> 00:12:18,825
And the rate-limited endpoint, with its 200s and 429s.

169
00:12:20,005 --> 00:12:21,882
These are the queries behind the panels:

170
00:12:22,162 --> 00:12:24,744
calls by status code, and the error ratio;

171
00:12:25,024 --> 00:12:29,333
histogram_quantile for p50 and p95 over five minutes;

172
00:12:29,613 --> 00:12:35,586
and in-flight calls and live sets. The HTTP side uses the same panels on the sets route.

173
00:12:36,766 --> 00:12:42,783
Everything is in the repository: code, OpenAPI document, proto and generation command,

174
00:12:42,783 --> 00:12:47,540
both clients, tests, Prometheus configuration and Grafana dashboard.

175
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:52,707
The README has the run and test commands, and the report has the tables and evidence.

176
00:12:52,707 --> 00:12:53,857
Thanks for watching.
